window.GRAMMAR = {"lessons": [{"id": "nominal", "title": "Sentences with no “is”", "sub": "البيت كبير — “the house (is) big”", "body": ["Palestinian Arabic has no word for “is / am / are” in the present. You just put the two things side by side: <b>البيت كبير</b> — literally “the house big” — means “the house is big.” Same with “I’m tired” (<b>أنا تعبان</b>) or “the coffee’s hot” (<b>القهوة سخنة</b>).", "This is one of the first things that makes you sound natural: don’t hunt for a verb where English has one. Subject, then the describing word, and you’re done."], "examples": [{"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["قريبة"]}, {"ar": "المسا بالبيت أحلى وقت.", "en": "The evening at home is the nicest time.", "src": "story-beg-22", "title": "The Evening", "hi": ["أحلى"]}, {"ar": "المي زرقا والرملة دافية.", "en": "The water is blue and the sand is warm.", "src": "story-beg-24", "title": "The Sea", "hi": ["زرقا", "دافية"]}, {"ar": "عندي كتاب حلو بأوضتي.", "en": "I have a nice book in my room.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["حلو"]}]}, {"id": "article", "title": "The definite article الـ", "sub": "“the”, glued to the front", "body": ["There’s one word for “the”: <b>الـ</b> (al-), stuck onto the front of the noun — <b>البيت</b> “the house”, <b>الولد</b> “the boy”.", "When the noun starts with a “sun letter” (ت ث د ذ ر ز س ش ص ض ط ظ ل ن), the <b>ل</b> of الـ assimilates: <b>الشمس</b> is written al-shams but <i>said</i> <b>ish-shams</b>, <b>الرمل</b> is <b>ir-raml</b>. With the other “moon letters”, you hear the l normally: <b>القمر</b> <b>il-qamar</b>. You don’t have to memorise the list — your ear picks it up fast."], "examples": [{"ar": "بروح عالمدرسة كل الصبح.", "en": "I go to school every morning.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["الصبح"]}, {"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["المدرسة"]}, {"ar": "بحب درس العربي والرسم.", "en": "I like Arabic class and drawing.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["العربي"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["الطاولة"]}]}, {"id": "pronouns", "title": "The people: I, you, he, she…", "sub": "subject pronouns", "body": ["The subject pronouns. Note that Palestinian keeps a separate “you” for men and women, and the verb usually already tells you who’s acting — so these often get dropped for emphasis only."], "tables": [{"title": "Subject pronouns", "rows": [["أنا", "ana", "I"], ["إنت", "inta", "you (m)"], ["إنتي", "inti", "you (f)"], ["هو", "huwwe", "he"], ["هي", "hiyye", "she"], ["إحنا", "i7na", "we"], ["إنتو", "intu", "you (pl)"], ["هم", "humme", "they"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "هو أحسن صاحب عندي.", "en": "He's my best friend.", "src": "story-beg-07", "title": "My Friend", "hi": ["هو"]}, {"ar": "أنا بحب الزيت والزعتر كتير.", "en": "I really like oil and za'tar.", "src": "story-beg-05", "title": "Breakfast", "hi": ["أنا"]}, {"ar": "أنا اسمي سامي وعندي عيلة كبيرة.", "en": "My name is Sami and I have a big family.", "src": "story-beg-02", "title": "My Family", "hi": ["أنا"]}, {"ar": "إحنا الولاد، كنا نساعد ونذوق سرا.", "en": "We kids used to help and taste secretly.", "src": "story-int-23", "title": "The Night of Eid", "hi": ["إحنا"]}]}, {"id": "bpresent", "title": "Everyday actions — the b- present", "sub": "بحب، بشرب، بروح", "body": ["For what you do regularly or are doing now, spoken Palestinian puts a little <b>بـ</b> (b-) on the front of the present verb: <b>بحب</b> “I like”, <b>بشرب</b> “I drink”, <b>بروح</b> “I go”. This is the everyday workhorse tense — most sentences you say will use it.", "The prefix shifts a bit by person — <b>بحب</b> (I), <b>بتحب</b> (you), <b>بيحب</b> (he), <b>بنحب</b> (we). The <b>Verbs</b> section conjugates all of these for any verb."], "examples": [{"ar": "بروح عالمدرسة كل الصبح.", "en": "I go to school every morning.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["بروح"]}, {"ar": "بحب درس العربي والرسم.", "en": "I like Arabic class and drawing.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["بحب"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["بنقعد"]}, {"ar": "بعد الغدا، بننام شوي.", "en": "After lunch, we nap a little.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["بننام"]}]}, {"id": "progressive", "title": "Right now — عم", "sub": "عم بشتغل — “I’m working (right now)”", "body": ["To stress that something is happening <i>right this moment</i>, put <b>عم</b> (3am) before the verb: <b>عم بشتغل</b> “I’m working”, <b>عم باكل</b> “I’m eating”. Without عم, the plain b- present covers both “I work” and “I’m working”; عم just makes the “right now” explicit."], "examples": [{"ar": "طار العصفور بالسما ونحنا عم نتفرج.", "en": "The bird flew into the sky while we watched.", "src": "story-int-28", "title": "The Bird", "hi": ["عم", "نتفرج"]}, {"ar": "مرة بالسوق، شفت ولد صغير عم يبكي لحاله.", "en": "Once at the market, I saw a little boy crying by himself.", "src": "story-int-16", "title": "The Boy Who Got Lost", "hi": ["عم", "يبكي"]}, {"ar": "وجّه المركب باتجاه إنجلترا، والوقت عم يركض.", "en": "He steered the ship toward England, with time running out.", "src": "book-atw80-ch35", "title": "Chapter 35 — Burning the Ship", "hi": ["عم", "يركض"]}, {"ar": "الكل حبس نفسه من الخوف وهو عم يعبر.", "en": "Everyone held their breath in fear as it crossed.", "src": "book-atw80-ch30", "title": "Chapter 30 — The Broken Bridge", "hi": ["عم", "يعبر"]}]}, {"id": "future", "title": "The future — رح", "sub": "رح أروح — “I’m going to go”", "body": ["For the future, put <b>رح</b> (ra7) — or its short form <b>حـ</b> stuck to the verb — before the <i>bare</i> present (no بـ): <b>رح أروح</b> / <b>حروح</b> “I’ll go”, <b>رح آكل</b> “I’ll eat”. Think of رح as “gonna”."], "examples": [{"ar": "الموظفة قالت إنه المكتبة رح تسكر.", "en": "The employee said the library was about to close.", "src": "story-int-30", "title": "A Day at the Library", "hi": ["رح", "تسكر"]}, {"ar": "مسكت إيده وقلتله ما تخاف، رح نلاقيها.", "en": "I held his hand and told him not to be scared, we'd find her.", "src": "story-int-16", "title": "The Boy Who Got Lost", "hi": ["رح", "نلاقيها"]}, {"ar": "قالت: رح أوحشكم كتير.", "en": "She said: I'll miss you all a lot.", "src": "story-adv-12", "title": "The Wedding Night", "hi": ["رح", "أوحشكم"]}, {"ar": "قال: قلتلك رح نضل أصحاب للأبد.", "en": "He said: I told you we'd stay friends forever.", "src": "story-adv-10", "title": "The Promise", "hi": ["رح", "نضل"]}]}, {"id": "negation", "title": "Saying no — ما and مش", "sub": "ما بحب · مش هون", "body": ["Two tools. To negate a <b>verb</b>, put <b>ما</b> (ma) in front: <b>ما بحب</b> “I don’t like”, <b>ما رحت</b> “I didn’t go.” In casual speech you’ll often hear a <b>ـش</b> tacked on the end too: <b>ما بحبش</b>.", "To negate <b>everything else</b> — a noun, an adjective, a place — use <b>مش</b> (mish): <b>مش هون</b> “not here”, <b>مش كبير</b> “not big”, <b>مش أنا</b> “not me.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "بالأول، ما كنت متحمس كتير.", "en": "At first, I wasn't very excited.", "src": "story-int-20", "title": "The Book I Read", "hi": ["ما"]}, {"ar": "فتشت بجيبي وبالشنطة بس ما لقيته.", "en": "I searched my pocket and my bag but didn't find it.", "src": "story-int-03", "title": "The Lost Key", "hi": ["ما"]}, {"ar": "بالأول ما كنت بعرف وين الأشيا.", "en": "At first I didn't know where things were.", "src": "story-int-02", "title": "First Day at Work", "hi": ["ما"]}, {"ar": "الأكل كان بيتهني والحكي ما وقف.", "en": "The food was delicious and the talking didn't stop.", "src": "story-int-21", "title": "A Lunch Invitation", "hi": ["ما"]}]}, {"id": "baddi", "title": "Wanting — بدّي", "sub": "بدّي أروح — “I want to go”", "body": ["“Want” isn’t a normal verb — it’s the little word <b>بدّ</b> plus an ending for who wants: <b>بدّي</b> “I want”, <b>بدّك</b> “you want”, <b>بدّه</b> “he wants.” Follow it with a bare present verb for “want to …”: <b>بدّي أروح</b> “I want to go”, <b>بدّك تاكل؟</b> “do you want to eat?”"], "tables": [{"title": "بدّ + ending = want", "rows": [["بدّي", "baddi", "I want"], ["بدّك", "baddak", "you want (m)"], ["بدّك", "baddik", "you want (f)"], ["بدّه", "baddo", "he wants"], ["بدّها", "baddha", "she wants"], ["بدّنا", "baddna", "we want"], ["بدّكم", "baddkom", "you want (pl)"], ["بدّهم", "baddhom", "they want"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "اليوم بدي أساعد إمي بالطبخ.", "en": "Today I want to help my mom cook.", "src": "story-beg-18", "title": "Cooking with Mom", "hi": ["بدي"]}, {"ar": "بدي أشتري خبز وحليب وبيض.", "en": "I want to buy bread, milk, and eggs.", "src": "story-beg-29", "title": "An Errand", "hi": ["بدي"]}, {"ar": "صحينا بكير لأنه القطاف بدو وقت.", "en": "We woke up early because picking takes time.", "src": "story-int-24", "title": "The Trip to the Orchard", "hi": ["بدو"]}, {"ar": "كل واحد كان بدو الأرض الأحسن.", "en": "Each one wanted the better land.", "src": "story-adv-06", "title": "The Two Brothers", "hi": ["بدو"]}]}, {"id": "indi", "title": "Having — عندي", "sub": "عندي وقت — “I have time”", "body": ["Same idea for “have”: there’s no verb, just <b>عند</b> (“at / by”) plus the ending — literally “at-me / at-you”: <b>عندي</b> “I have”, <b>عندك</b> “you have”, <b>عنده</b> “he has.” <b>عندي وقت</b> “I have time”, <b>عندك سيارة؟</b> “do you have a car?”"], "tables": [{"title": "عند + ending = have", "rows": [["عندي", "3indi", "I have"], ["عندك", "3indak", "you have (m)"], ["عندك", "3indik", "you have (f)"], ["عنده", "3indo", "he has"], ["عندها", "3indha", "she has"], ["عندنا", "3indna", "we have"], ["عندكم", "3indkom", "you have (pl)"], ["عندهم", "3indhom", "they have"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "عندي كتاب حلو بأوضتي.", "en": "I have a nice book in my room.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["عندي"]}, {"ar": "هو أحسن صاحب عندي.", "en": "He's my best friend.", "src": "story-beg-07", "title": "My Friend", "hi": ["عندي"]}, {"ar": "جارنا عنده كلب اسمه لولو.", "en": "Our neighbor has a dog named Lulu.", "src": "story-beg-26", "title": "The Dog", "hi": ["عنده"]}, {"ar": "عندي أخ وأخت، وهمة أصغر مني.", "en": "I have a brother and a sister, and they're younger than me.", "src": "story-beg-02", "title": "My Family", "hi": ["عندي"]}]}, {"id": "possessive", "title": "Mine, yours, his — endings on nouns", "sub": "بيتي، بيتك، بيته", "body": ["To say “my / your / his …”, you don’t use a separate word — you stick an ending on the noun, the same endings as بدّي and عندي: <b>بيت</b> “house” → <b>بيتي</b> “my house”, <b>بيتك</b> “your house”, <b>بيته</b> “his house”, <b>بيتنا</b> “our house.”"], "tables": [{"title": "بيت (house) + ending", "rows": [["بيتي", "beeti", "my house"], ["بيتك", "beetak", "your house (m)"], ["بيتك", "beetik", "your house (f)"], ["بيته", "beeto", "his house"], ["بيتها", "beetha", "her house"], ["بيتنا", "beetna", "our house"], ["بيتكم", "beetkom", "your house (pl)"], ["بيتهم", "beethom", "their house"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["بيتنا"]}, {"ar": "أم أحمد بتسكن جنبنا.", "en": "Um Ahmad lives next to us.", "src": "story-beg-12", "title": "The Neighbors", "hi": ["جنبنا"]}, {"ar": "عندي كتاب حلو بأوضتي.", "en": "I have a nice book in my room.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["عندي"]}, {"ar": "بسكر عيوني وبنام مبسوط.", "en": "I close my eyes and sleep happy.", "src": "story-beg-30", "title": "The Night", "hi": ["عيوني"]}]}, {"id": "questions", "title": "Asking things", "sub": "شو، وين، ليش…", "body": ["The question words. Word order stays the same as a statement — you just drop the question word in, usually at the front: <b>وين رايح؟</b> “where are you going?”, <b>شو بدّك؟</b> “what do you want?”"], "tables": [{"title": "Question words", "rows": [["شو", "shu", "what"], ["مين", "miin", "who"], ["وين", "ween", "where"], ["إيمتى", "eemta", "when"], ["ليش", "leesh", "why"], ["كيف", "kiif", "how"], ["قدّيش", "addeesh", "how much"], ["كم", "kam", "how many"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "قربت منه وسألته وين إمه.", "en": "I got close to him and asked where his mom was.", "src": "story-int-16", "title": "The Boy Who Got Lost", "hi": ["وين"]}, {"ar": "بالأول ما كنت بعرف وين الأشيا.", "en": "At first I didn't know where things were.", "src": "story-int-02", "title": "First Day at Work", "hi": ["وين"]}, {"ar": "خفت كتير وما عرفت شو أقول.", "en": "I got very scared and didn't know what to say.", "src": "story-int-27", "title": "The Broken Cup", "hi": ["شو"]}, {"ar": "سأل شو المشكلة وحاول يساعد أبوي.", "en": "He asked what the problem was and tried to help my dad.", "src": "story-int-11", "title": "The Car That Broke Down", "hi": ["شو"]}]}, {"id": "demonstratives", "title": "This and these", "sub": "هاد، هاي، هدول", "body": ["“This” changes with gender, “these” doesn’t: <b>هاد</b> (haad) for a masculine thing, <b>هاي</b> (hayy) for a feminine one, <b>هدول</b> (hadool) for plural. <b>هاد الولد</b> “this boy”, <b>هاي البنت</b> “this girl”, <b>هدول الناس</b> “these people.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "رجعت عالضيعة، بس هالمرة كمعلمة.", "en": "She returned to the village, but this time as a teacher.", "src": "story-adv-07", "title": "The Girl Who Loved to Learn", "hi": ["هالمرة"]}, {"ar": "العيلة كلها زعلت من هالوضع.", "en": "The whole family was saddened by the situation.", "src": "story-adv-06", "title": "The Two Brothers", "hi": ["هالوضع"]}, {"ar": "قال: هالأرض عمرتها بعرق جبيني.", "en": "He said: I built up this land with the sweat of my brow.", "src": "story-adv-15", "title": "The Last Harvest", "hi": ["هالأرض"]}, {"ar": "قال: هاي مكافأة على أمانتك.", "en": "He said: this is a reward for your integrity.", "src": "story-adv-22", "title": "The Debt", "hi": ["هاي"]}]}, {"id": "relative", "title": "The one that… — اللي", "sub": "الولد اللي بيلعب", "body": ["One little word does the job of “who / which / that”: <b>اللي</b> (illi), for people and things alike. <b>الولد اللي بيلعب</b> “the boy who’s playing”, <b>الأكل اللي طبخته</b> “the food that I cooked.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "الولاد كانوا يلعبوا ويجمعوا الزيتون اللي وقع.", "en": "The kids were playing and gathering the olives that fell.", "src": "story-int-24", "title": "The Trip to the Orchard", "hi": ["اللي"]}, {"ar": "ضحك وقال: البحر بيحترم اللي بيحترمه.", "en": "He laughed and said: the sea respects those who respect it.", "src": "story-adv-13", "title": "The Fisherman", "hi": ["اللي"]}, {"ar": "قال: الله يجازي اللي عمل هالخير.", "en": "He said: may God reward whoever did this good deed.", "src": "story-adv-14", "title": "The Poor Neighbor", "hi": ["اللي"]}, {"ar": "أبوي قال: الخير اللي بتعمله، بيرجعلك.", "en": "My dad said: the good you do comes back to you.", "src": "story-adv-03", "title": "The Stranger Guest", "hi": ["اللي"]}]}, {"id": "kaan", "title": "Was and were — كان", "sub": "كان تعبان — “he was tired”", "body": ["Remember there’s no “is” in the present — but there <i>is</i> a past “was / were”: <b>كان</b> (kaan). <b>كان تعبان</b> “he was tired”, <b>كنت هون</b> “I was here”, <b>كانوا مبسوطين</b> “they were happy.” Put كان before a present verb and you get “used to / was …-ing”: <b>كنت بلعب</b> “I used to play.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "كان خايف وجناحه موجوع.", "en": "It was scared and its wing was hurt.", "src": "story-int-28", "title": "The Bird", "hi": ["كان"]}, {"ar": "المكتبة كانت كبيرة وهادية.", "en": "The library was big and quiet.", "src": "story-int-30", "title": "A Day at the Library", "hi": ["كانت"]}, {"ar": "كان يوم ما بننساه.", "en": "It was a day we won't forget.", "src": "story-int-01", "title": "A Trip to the Sea", "hi": ["كان"]}, {"ar": "جدتي كانت أسعد وحدة بالعرس.", "en": "My grandmother was the happiest one at the wedding.", "src": "story-int-04", "title": "My Cousin's Wedding", "hi": ["كانت"]}]}, {"id": "past", "title": "Telling what happened — the past", "sub": "رحت، أكلت، شفت", "body": ["Stories run on the past tense, and it’s built by changing the <i>end</i> of the verb instead of the front: <b>راح</b> “he went”, <b>رحت</b> “I went”, <b>رحنا</b> “we went.” No بـ here — the past never takes it.", "The full set of endings, for any verb and any pattern, is in the <b>Verbs</b> section — open a verb and look at the “Past” column."], "examples": [{"ar": "الشوارع صارت مليانة مي.", "en": "The streets got full of water.", "src": "story-beg-16", "title": "The Rain", "hi": ["صارت"]}, {"ar": "اليوم إمي طبخت ملوخية ورز.", "en": "Today my mom cooked mulukhiyah and rice.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["طبخت"]}, {"ar": "اليوم نزلت شتا من الصبح.", "en": "Today it rained from the morning.", "src": "story-beg-16", "title": "The Rain", "hi": ["نزلت"]}, {"ar": "قعدت بالبيت وشربت شاي سخن.", "en": "I stayed home and drank hot tea.", "src": "story-beg-16", "title": "The Rain", "hi": ["قعدت"]}]}, {"id": "comparative", "title": "Bigger, better — comparatives", "sub": "أكبر من · أحسن", "body": ["To compare, most adjectives take an <b>أ-</b> shape: <b>كبير</b> “big” → <b>أكبر</b> “bigger”, <b>حلو</b> → <b>أحلى</b> “sweeter/nicer”, <b>كتير</b> → <b>أكتر</b> “more.” Add <b>من</b> for “than”: <b>أكبر من بيتنا</b> “bigger than our house.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "المسا بالبيت أحلى وقت.", "en": "The evening at home is the nicest time.", "src": "story-beg-22", "title": "The Evening", "hi": ["أحلى"]}, {"ar": "هو أحسن صاحب عندي.", "en": "He's my best friend.", "src": "story-beg-07", "title": "My Friend", "hi": ["أحسن"]}, {"ar": "الخبز السخن أحلى شي.", "en": "Hot bread is the nicest thing.", "src": "story-beg-19", "title": "The Bakery", "hi": ["أحلى"]}, {"ar": "الشاي بالنعنع أحلى شي بالمسا.", "en": "Mint tea is the nicest thing in the evening.", "src": "story-beg-09", "title": "Tea", "hi": ["أحلى"]}]}, {"id": "fi", "title": "“There is / there are” — في (and “in” is بـ)", "sub": "في أكل · ما في وقت · بالبيت", "body": ["This one confuses English speakers because <b>في</b> <i>looks</i> like it should mean “in” — and in Modern Standard Arabic it does. But in spoken Palestinian, <b>في</b> (fi) usually means <b>“there is / there are”</b>: <b>في أكل</b> “there’s food”, <b>في ناس كتير</b> “there are a lot of people.” The negative is <b>ما في</b>: <b>ما في وقت</b> “there’s no time.”", "So how do you say <b>“in / at”</b>? Palestinian sticks <b>بـ</b> (b-) onto the noun instead: <b>بالبيت</b> “in the house”, <b>بالسيارة</b> “in the car”, <b>بالشغل</b> “at work.” Keep the two apart — <b>في</b> = there is, <b>بـ</b> = in — and في stops feeling random."], "examples": [{"ar": "فيه قصص وصور كتير.", "en": "It has stories and many pictures.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["فيه"]}, {"ar": "فيها شجر زيتون وليمون.", "en": "It has olive and lemon trees.", "src": "story-beg-10", "title": "The Garden", "hi": ["فيها"]}, {"ar": "فيها سرير وطاولة وكرسي.", "en": "It has a bed, a table, and a chair.", "src": "story-beg-17", "title": "My Room", "hi": ["فيها"]}, {"ar": "عالحيط في صورة للبحر.", "en": "On the wall there is a picture of the sea.", "src": "story-beg-17", "title": "My Room", "hi": ["في"]}]}, {"id": "idafa", "title": "No word for “of” — just stack the nouns", "sub": "باب البيت = the door of the house", "body": ["English links nouns with “of” or “’s” (“the door <i>of</i> the house”, “the house<i>’s</i> door”). Arabic just sets the two nouns next to each other, the owned thing first, with <b>no</b> word for “of”: <b>باب البيت</b> “the door of the house”, <b>بيت الرجل</b> “the man’s house”, <b>اسم البنت</b> “the girl’s name.”", "Watch the “the”: only the <i>second</i> noun takes الـ — <b>باب البيت</b>, literally “door the-house” (never الباب البيت). For a looser “belonging to,” spoken Palestinian also has <b>تبع</b>: <b>الكتاب تبع أحمد</b> “Ahmad’s book.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "بحب درس العربي والرسم.", "en": "I like Arabic class and drawing.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["درس", "العربي"]}, {"ar": "بحب صوت المطر عالشباك.", "en": "I like the sound of rain on the window.", "src": "story-beg-08", "title": "The Weather Today", "hi": ["صوت", "المطر"]}, {"ar": "يوم الجمعة بروح عالسوق.", "en": "On Friday I go to the market.", "src": "story-beg-06", "title": "To the Market", "hi": ["يوم", "الجمعة"]}, {"ar": "بحب ليالي الصيف الطويلة.", "en": "I like the long summer nights.", "src": "story-beg-28", "title": "Summer", "hi": ["ليالي", "الصيف"]}]}, {"id": "gender", "title": "Masculine & feminine", "sub": "ولد كبير · بنت كبيرة", "body": ["Arabic sorts every noun as masculine or feminine, and the words around it must match — something English never does. The usual sign of feminine is a <b>ة</b> ending: <b>بنت</b> “girl”, <b>سيارة</b> “car”, <b>قهوة</b> “coffee” are feminine; most other nouns are masculine.", "Adjectives and verbs agree with it. Masculine <b>ولد كبير</b> “a big boy” → feminine <b>بنت كبيرة</b> “a big girl” (add ة). <b>هو راح</b> “he went” → <b>هي راحت</b> “she went.” Learn each noun’s gender together with the word — there’s no shortcut, but the ة gives most of them away."], "examples": [{"ar": "بروح عالمدرسة كل الصبح.", "en": "I go to school every morning.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["عالمدرسة"]}, {"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["قريبة"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["الطاولة"]}, {"ar": "بحب أشتري خضرة طازة.", "en": "I like to buy fresh vegetables.", "src": "story-beg-25", "title": "The Vegetables", "hi": ["خضرة"]}]}]};
